 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server commits each edit as one event revision and pushes committed revisions to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets. Browsers display a revisioned view of the board and resume from their last applied revision after a reconnect.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The project operates its own connection capacity, reconnect handling, and revision replay in exchange for sub-second delivery and no dependence on a realtime vendor's client model or recurring fees. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it repeats reads for every browser and adds seconds of lag. A hosted realtime service was rejected because it adds recurring fees and couples the board's delivery to one provider.
+
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice: change storage keeps replayable per-event revisions and publishes each committed revision to delivery without loss; delivery holds the socket sessions and fans revisions out; browser sync reconnects and replays from the last applied revision.
 
